_GUIDE_PLAN_SPEC = """Plan JSON (valid JSON only, no markdown fence):
{
  "goal": "the user's task in one line",
  "goal_generic": "the same task without personal names/numbers/text/addresses (so that it would work for anyone else doing it)",
  "app": {"name": "app name"} or null,
  "ui_lang": "bn | en | mixed",
  "success_signal": "proof that the task is finished — what will be visible on screen",
  "traps": ["intermediate stations that are wrong to treat as the destination (1-3)"],
  "steps": [
    {"i": 1, "act": "tap|type|scroll|back|open_app|intent",
     "target": "the meaning of the target — both the English name and the name in the app's own language + the alternative names/looks it may have in the app (for example \"Settings & privacy → Settings\", \"menu ≡ three lines\")",
     "expect": "which screen/state will appear after this step",
     "guide": "one short conversational sentence to tell the user, in the user's language",
     "reversible": true}
  ]
}
Rules:
- reversible by meaning: sending, deleting, payment, post/share, changing an account/password, submit/confirm → false; scrolling, opening a menu, entering a page → true.
- The fewest steps, at most 15; no extra confirmation/back steps.
- If opening an app or going to a specific settings page is directly possible, that is the first step (open_app / intent) — not a step of looking for an icon on the screen.
- Put in traps any near-named thing that would lead the wrong way if tapped (for example if the target is "Settings", then "Profile" or "Personal details") — later, when choosing by looking at the screen, this will stop the wrong button.
- Do not keep personal information in goal_generic and guide.
- Do not create a step that fills in a password/OTP/payment itself; in that step tell the user in guide to do it themselves."""

